From e89f1b1baa8107de6a32588729dcd395368e0f88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <ms178@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 21:30:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] aarch64, riscv64: plain char is unsigned per target ABI
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Plain `char` was compiled as SIGNED on every target — an x86-64 default
leaked into AArch64 and RISC-V64, where the SysV ABIs specify an
unsigned plain char. Every `char c = 200` read back -56 instead of 200,
every narrowing `(int)(char)x` sign-extended instead of zero-extending,
and character constants kept their sign (C11 6.4.4.4p10) — so
declaration and constant DISAGREED, e.g. tests/regression/
cast_chain_fold.c produced a different hash than GCC (caught by the
ARM differential suite).

The distinction is resolved at the parse boundary: the parser is the
only layer that can still tell plain `char` (target-defined rank) from
explicit `signed char`, and TypeSpecifier::Char now unambiguously means
explicit `signed char` — sema CType mapping, IR lowering (I8 vs U8
decides load/store extension), and typeof/typedef round-trips stay
flag-free. The driver records the target default process-globally
before parsing (one target per driver binary); -fsigned-char /
-funsigned-char override it and also flip character-constant values,
matching GCC on both an x86 -funsigned-char and an AArch64
-fsigned-char probe.

Validation: char_signedness_target.c new, passes on x86-64, AArch64 and
RISC-V64 with and without both flags (GCC parity on all); cast_chain_fold
now matches GCC on AArch64; lib 1273/0/6; x86 536/5 (same 5 pre-existing);
ARM 378/30/75 (was 376/31; also fixes the char probes in the wide-vaarg
work); RISC-V va_arg_wide_struct re-verified rc=0.
---
